# Copy to .env and fill in. NOTHING here goes to git with real values.

# --- Released version ---
# The tag the five application images run at. `scripts/build-images.sh` sets
# it to the current short commit SHA and prints the line to paste here.
#
# THIS IS THE ROLLBACK CONTROL. To roll back a bad deploy, set this to the
# previous SHA and run `docker compose up -d` -- retagging, not rebuilding.
# Left unset, compose falls back to `:dev`, which is fine for local work and
# is NOT a deployable state: `:dev` names whatever was last built here.
IT_SUITE_VERSION=dev

# --- Host bind address ---
# The host IP Traefik publishes :443 and :8443 on. Leave at 0.0.0.0 on a
# dedicated host.
#
# SET IT TO A SPECIFIC ADDRESS WHEN THIS HOST ALREADY RUNS SOMETHING ON :443.
# Linux refuses a wildcard bind for a port that any specific address already
# holds, so 0.0.0.0 does not merely share the port badly -- the container fails
# to start with "address already in use". Read the existing listener's bind
# address first (`scripts/host-check.sh` section 2 prints it): if the other
# platform shows 0.0.0.0:443 it holds the port everywhere and one of the two
# must move; if it shows a specific address, put ITMS on a different one here.
#
# Whatever you set must match the hostname in ITS_S3_PUBLIC_ENDPOINT,
# ITS_GATEWAY_URL and your TLS certificate -- agents and browsers resolve a
# name, and a name that resolves to the other address reaches the other
# platform.
ITMS_BIND_IP=0.0.0.0

# The subnet ITMS's own container network uses. Change it only if this default
# collides with a real network at your site.
#
# It is pinned rather than left to Docker because Docker's default pool is
# 172.16.0.0/12 in /16 slices -- 172.16 through 172.31 -- so a site using, say,
# 172.22.x for its client subnets can have Docker hand a container network the
# same range as the endpoints this platform manages. Containers then blackhole
# exactly the machines they are meant to reach, and it presents as a routing or
# firewall fault long before anyone suspects the container runtime.
ITMS_NETWORK_SUBNET=172.19.240.0/24

# --- Deployment mode ---
# "production" fails closed on missing key material (signing key, CA root,
# audit HMAC key) instead of silently generating it, and (with multi-tenant)
# requires the org claim on every token. Use "dev" only for local development.
ITS_ENVIRONMENT=production
# Reject operator tokens that lack the org claim instead of using the default
# org. Leave false for single-org on-prem; set true for multi-tenant.
ITS_MULTI_TENANT=false

# --- PostgreSQL ---
POSTGRES_ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me
APP_DB_PASSWORD=change-me
# End-user portal DB role (portal_user, RLS-scoped to the requester's own
# tickets -- migration 0028). Consumed by the portal API (backend/app/modules/
# portal/api.py) and the portal UI (frontend/src/modules/portal/) -- both land
# on this branch. See ITS_DATABASE_PORTAL_URL below, which is the setting that
# actually wires this password to a connection; without it every portal route
# 500s on first use (Settings.database_portal_url defaults to "" so the many
# pre-existing test modules that build Settings directly don't all need it).
PORTAL_DB_PASSWORD=change-me

# Backend database URLs (asyncpg driver)
ITS_DATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://app_user:${APP_DB_PASSWORD}@postgres:5432/itsuite
ITS_DATABASE_ADMIN_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://its_admin:${POSTGRES_ADMIN_PASSWORD}@postgres:5432/itsuite
# End-user portal: RLS-scoped as portal_user, never app_user -- portal_user's
# row-scoped (requester-level) policies do not apply to app_user's connection.
# REQUIRED for every /api/v1/portal/* route; omitting this is a 500 on first
# request, not a startup failure (see app/modules/portal/api.py::_portal_engine).
# On an EXISTING deployment being upgraded to this branch, also see the
# "End-user portal" section of deploy/README.md -- the role is created NOLOGIN
# by migration 0028, and this URL cannot authenticate until an operator gives
# it a LOGIN password by hand.
ITS_DATABASE_PORTAL_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://portal_user:${PORTAL_DB_PASSWORD}@postgres:5432/itsuite

# --- Redis (Celery broker + gateway pub/sub) ---
# The api/gateway/worker services also set this explicitly in docker-compose.yml;
# it is listed here so a non-compose deployment (or `docker run` against this
# env file) does not silently fall back to Settings' localhost default.
ITS_REDIS_URL=redis://redis:6379/0

# --- Keycloak / OIDC ---
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_USER=admin
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me
ITS_OIDC_ISSUER=https://your-host/realms/it-suite
ITS_OIDC_JWKS_URL=http://keycloak:8080/realms/it-suite/protocol/openid-connect/certs
ITS_OIDC_AUDIENCE=it-suite
ITS_DEFAULT_ORG_SLUG=toeshee
# Token claim carrying the organization slug. Change only if your IdP mapper
# emits it under a different name than the realm import's `org` mapper.
ITS_OIDC_ORG_CLAIM=org
# OPTIONAL. The client the console's own web app signs in with; the API admits
# only tokens issued to it (`azp`). Defaults to it-suite-web. Must match
# VITE_OIDC_CLIENT_ID if that is ever changed.
# ITS_OIDC_CONSOLE_CLIENT_ID=it-suite-web

# Microsoft / Entra sign-in (OPTIONAL). `deploy/keycloak/realm-it-suite.json`
# ships the provider with an EMPTY clientSecret on purpose: that file is in
# git, and a secret committed there is a credential in every clone and every CI
# cache, rotatable only by rewriting history.
#
# These values reach the LIVE realm through the admin console, following
# deploy/README.md "Branding and extending sign-in". Nothing reads them
# automatically — they are here so the operator has one place to keep them.
ENTRA_TENANT_ID=
ENTRA_CLIENT_ID=
ENTRA_CLIENT_SECRET=

# The identity provider's admin API: one in-network URL, two service clients.
#
# NONE OF THIS IS AN OPERATOR STEP ANY MORE, which is why the values below ship
# SET rather than blank-and-commented. `deploy/keycloak/init-clients.py` runs as
# the `keycloak-init` job on every `docker compose up`: it creates both clients,
# grants each exactly its roles, and writes both secrets to the shared secrets
# volume, mode 600. It is idempotent, and re-running it (`docker compose up
# keycloak-init`) is its normal case, not a recovery.
#
# THE SECRETS ARE FILE PATHS, NOT SECRETS -- same rule as
# ITS_AUDIT_HMAC_KEY_PATH. An env var holding a credential is readable by
# `docker inspect`, lands in shell history, and is copied into every process's
# environment. The two paths must match the filenames the init job writes;
# `backend/tests/test_keycloak_init_job.py` holds it to that.
#
# NEVER KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD HERE. That account can create any user and reset
# any password in the realm. The init job uses it once and then rotates it to a
# random value only the secrets volume holds, and nothing in normal operation
# authenticates as it again -- deploy/GO-LIVE.md §2.
#
# THE URL IS SHARED BY BOTH CLIENTS; there is no second copy of it.
# `http://keycloak:8080` is the in-network address and never goes out through
# Traefik, which is what lets the admin API stay off the public entrypoint
# entirely.
ITS_KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_URL=http://keycloak:8080
ITS_KEYCLOAK_REALM=it-suite

# Credential events: the EVENTS client -- `view-events`, and nothing else (deploy/README.md,
# "Forwarding Keycloak's credential events"). It copies four credential events
# into the ITMS hash-chained audit log hourly: passkey registered, passkey
# removed, identity linked, identity unlinked. Its worst case is an information
# leak, which is precisely why it is a separate client from the one below and
# not a second role on it.
#
# Blank ITS_KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_URL above and the sweep becomes a no-op, with the
# platform status page reporting `keycloak_events: configured = false` so the
# gap is visible rather than silent. Note what else that switches off: the URL
# is shared, so blanking it also turns off user provisioning below.
ITS_KEYCLOAK_EVENTS_CLIENT_ID=it-suite-events
ITS_KEYCLOAK_EVENTS_SECRET_PATH=/var/lib/its/secrets/keycloak-events.secret

# User lifecycle: the PROVISIONING client -- `manage-users` + `manage-realm`
# (deploy/README.md, "The provisioning client"). Required before
# the console can create, disable, password-reset or delete a user, AND before
# the first-boot setup screen can create the very first administrator. Leave
# either of the two lines below unset and the very first thing a buyer does --
# claim the installation -- is refused with "identity provisioning is not
# configured".
#
# IT SHIPS SET, WHICH REVERSES WHAT THIS FILE USED TO SAY, and the reason is
# that the world it described is gone. This block used to ship the path
# COMMENTED OUT and tell you to create the client in the admin console, write
# its secret to a file, and THEN uncomment. `keycloak-init` now does all three
# on every `docker compose up`, so the old instruction described work nobody
# does any more -- while the commented-out line quietly made a fresh install
# refuse its own setup screen.
#
# THE THREE-WAY CONTRACT IS UNCHANGED, and it is still worth knowing, because
# the middle branch is what a cold host passes through
# (`KeycloakAdminConfig.from_settings`):
#
#   URL or path EMPTY       -> "not configured". The lifecycle routes answer
#                              503 naming the setting, and the setup screen
#                              says provisioning is not configured. Supported,
#                              but no longer the shipped state.
#   set, file MISSING/empty -> RAISES (FileNotFoundError, or ValueError on an
#                              empty file). Deliberate, and NOT softened: an
#                              operator who set the path believes provisioning
#                              is running, and degrading to "not configured"
#                              would hide that. The setup route alone reads
#                              that state as "the init job has not finished"
#                              and answers 503 asking you to try again --
#                              which on a first boot is exactly what it is.
#   set, file PRESENT       -> provisioning works. This is the steady state,
#                              seconds after `docker compose up`.
#
# Note the first branch needs the URL *or* the path to be empty, and the URL is
# SHARED with the event sweep above -- so blanking the path alone, on a
# deployment that forwards events, lands you in the second branch and not the
# first.
#
# READ THE COST. `manage-users` has NO finer split in Keycloak: this credential
# can create any user and reset any password in the realm. Whoever owns ITMS
# owns every identity in it. `manage-realm` is what lets Settings -> Email write
# the realm's SMTP block. The full accounting is in
# docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-19-invisible-identity-layer-design.md §4.
#
# THE FIRST-BOOT WINDOW IS EXPECTED AND SELF-CLEARING. `keycloak-init`
# deliberately does not gate the `api` service (docker-compose.yml: making it a
# dependency would turn an init failure into a product that will not start at
# all), so for a short while after a cold `docker compose up` the path below is
# set and the file is not written yet. In that window the setup screen answers
# "still starting up ... try again" and the status page reports
# `keycloak_events` as `down / misconfigured`. Both clear themselves when the
# job finishes. Neither is a reason to edit these two lines -- check
# `docker compose ps -a keycloak-init` instead (GO-LIVE.md §2).
ITS_KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_CLIENT_ID=it-suite-provisioning
ITS_KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_SECRET_PATH=/var/lib/its/secrets/keycloak-admin.secret

# The console email-domain allowlist (deploy/README.md, "The email-domain
# allowlist"). An address is admitted iff its domain EQUALS this value or ends
# with "." + this value, both lowercased. Enforced at all three doors that turn
# a token into a principal: the HTTP API (403), the console WebSocket (close
# 4403) and the remote-control tunnel (close 4403).
#
# LEAVE IT EMPTY UNLESS YOU MEAN IT. Empty means NO RESTRICTION, which is the
# behaviour of every deployment that predates this setting -- and that default
# is load-bearing, not laziness: any other value here would refuse every token
# on every existing deployment the moment it upgraded. A security control that
# arrives switched on by surprise is an outage.
#
# BEFORE SETTING IT, RUN THE PREFLIGHT. Turning it on refuses everyone whose
# email fails the rule INCLUDING YOUR OWN ACCOUNT, and the console is the thing
# that would have to let you back in:
#
#   ITS_DATABASE_ADMIN_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://its_admin:...@localhost/itsuite \
#     backend/.venv/bin/python scripts/preflight_email_domain.py toeshee.com
#
# Break-glass if it happens anyway: blank this line and restart the api --
# settings are read once at create_app, so editing .env alone changes nothing.
ITS_CONSOLE_EMAIL_DOMAIN=

# --- OpenBao (signing + PKI + credential vault). Use "local" backends only
# for development. ---
ITS_SIGNING_BACKEND=openbao
ITS_CA_BACKEND=openbao
# The credential/secrets vault (backend/app/core/vault.py) -- stored
# credentials, backup repository keys, license keys, and provisioning
# answer-file secrets are all envelope-encrypted through this backend
# (invariant #3: never plaintext in the app DB). Left unset, it silently
# defaults to "local" (a root key file on the api/gateway/worker container's
# own disk instead of OpenBao) even though every other backend here is
# already "openbao" and deploy/openbao/init.sh already provisions the
# `transit` mount this backend uses (per-org keys are created on first use,
# same mount as the job-signing key). Setting this to "local" in production
# is a real, silent downgrade of invariant #3, not a convenience default --
# make sure it is "openbao" here.
ITS_VAULT_BACKEND=openbao
ITS_OPENBAO_ADDR=http://openbao:8200
# TWO DIFFERENT TOKENS, deliberately -- they were the same value until the
# first-boot runbook was executed for real, which handed every application
# process a token that can re-mount, re-key or delete the job-signing key.
#
# ITS_OPENBAO_TOKEN is what the api/gateway/worker use at runtime. Give it the
# LEAST-PRIVILEGE `its-app` policy from deploy/README.md first-boot step 3.4 --
# sign with job-signing, envelope-encrypt per org, sign/revoke agent certs, and
# nothing else. It is NOT the root token.
ITS_OPENBAO_TOKEN=change-me
# OPENBAO_TOKEN is consumed ONLY by the one-shot `openbao-init` service, which
# enables the `transit` and `pki` mounts -- that needs sys/mounts, which the
# runtime policy above deliberately does not have. Use the root token here for
# first boot, then blank this line out again (the service has already run) and
# store the root token offline. See deploy/GO-LIVE.md.
OPENBAO_TOKEN=change-me
ITS_CA_DIR=/var/lib/its/ca
# Only read by the "local" (development) backends above; ignored entirely when
# the backend is "openbao". Leave both unset in production -- with
# ITS_ENVIRONMENT=production the local backends refuse to auto-generate key
# material rather than silently minting a new signing key or CA.
# ITS_SIGNING_KEY_PATH=/var/lib/its/secrets/job-signing.key
# ITS_VAULT_ROOT_KEY_PATH=/var/lib/its/secrets/vault-root.key

# --- Audit-log authentication ---
# HMAC key that authenticates the audit chain so a SQL-write primitive cannot
# forge valid-looking events. Required when ITS_ENVIRONMENT=production: the api
# and gateway load it during startup and REFUSE TO START without it.
# The path must be on the shared `its-secrets` volume, which both the api and
# the gateway mount -- the two processes must load the SAME BYTES or they
# compute different chain links for the same org and GET /api/v1/audit/verify
# fails. Provision it once, before step 5 of deploy/README.md's first boot:
#   docker compose run --rm --no-deps --entrypoint sh api -c \
#     'head -c 32 /dev/urandom > /var/lib/its/secrets/audit-hmac.key && \
#      chmod 600 /var/lib/its/secrets/audit-hmac.key'
# Back it up with the CA (docs/07-backup-restore.md) -- restoring the database
# without this key makes the whole restored chain unverifiable.
ITS_AUDIT_HMAC_KEY_PATH=/var/lib/its/secrets/audit-hmac.key

# --- Object storage ---
# Also holds the backup engine's per-tenant repositories under the
# {org_id}/backup/{repository_id}/ prefix (chunks + encrypted manifests).
# Backups add NO new required env vars: they reuse this object storage, the
# vault (repository master keys are sealed via OpenBao, above), and the Redis
# used by the Celery worker for the backup window/retention-gc/verification
# sweeps. Size the bucket for backup retention; there is no cross-org dedup.
# Provisioning & imaging likewise reuses this object storage under the
# {org_id}/provision/ prefix (image/build payloads + short-lived rendered
# answer files), the vault (answer-file secrets are sealed via OpenBao), the
# Redis worker (provision-enroll-dispatch / provision-timeout sweeps), and the
# enrollment CA — it adds NO new required env vars and NO new compose services.
MINIO_ROOT_USER=its-minio
MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD=change-me
ITS_S3_ACCESS_KEY=${MINIO_ROOT_USER}
ITS_S3_SECRET_KEY=${MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD}
ITS_S3_BUCKET=it-suite
# TWO ENDPOINTS FOR ONE BUCKET. They are not interchangeable and one of them
# is not optional.
#
# ITS_S3_ENDPOINT is where the API, gateway and worker read and write objects.
# Unset, boto3 defaults to the PUBLIC AWS S3 endpoint rather than MinIO, so
# every object read/write leaves the cluster and fails. The api and worker
# services also set this explicitly in docker-compose.yml; it is listed here so
# a non-compose deployment gets it too. Point it at a real S3-compatible
# endpoint if you are not using the bundled MinIO.
ITS_S3_ENDPOINT=http://minio:9000
# ITS_S3_PUBLIC_ENDPOINT is where an ENDPOINT reaches the same bucket, and it
# is used for exactly one thing: signing presigned URLs. It is REQUIRED with
# ITS_ENVIRONMENT=production -- the api and worker refuse to touch object
# storage without it, deliberately.
#
# Why it cannot be left to fall back to the value above: an agent has no route
# to `minio` and no DNS that resolves it. SigV4 signs the host and the full
# path, so the URL an agent receives must have been signed against the name the
# agent will resolve; there is no fixing it up afterwards. Signing against the
# internal name produces an app.deploy job that dispatches cleanly, reaches the
# endpoint, and dies at the download with a name-resolution error you cannot
# see from the control plane. That is not hypothetical -- it is the defect this
# setting exists to close.
#
# SCHEME AND HOST ONLY. No path, no trailing bucket name: the bucket is already
# the first path segment of a path-style S3 URL, and it is what the `minio`
# router in docker-compose.yml matches on. A path here is refused at startup
# because a proxy prefix cannot be made to work -- strip it and every signature
# is invalid, keep it and the prefix is read as the bucket name.
#
# `https://` is required in production: a presigned URL is a bearer credential
# for the object it names until it expires.
#
# WITH THE BUNDLED MINIO this is your own :443 hostname -- the same one in
# ITS_OIDC_ISSUER -- because docker-compose.yml routes `/<bucket>/` on :443
# straight to MinIO. Nothing else to configure; step 1 of README.md's first
# boot rewrites `your-host` here along with everywhere else.
ITS_S3_PUBLIC_ENDPOINT=https://your-host
# WITH EXTERNAL OBJECT STORAGE (real S3, Wasabi, a MinIO cluster of your own),
# the two endpoints are genuinely different hosts and neither is this server:
#
#   ITS_S3_ENDPOINT=https://s3.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com
#   ITS_S3_PUBLIC_ENDPOINT=https://s3.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com
#
# Usually the same value twice -- an external store is already reachable from
# both sides, and the split exists because the BUNDLED one is not. Set the
# internal one to a VPC/private endpoint if your provider has one; that is the
# case where they differ and the reason both are configurable. In this
# configuration the `minio` router in docker-compose.yml routes nothing (no
# request carries your bucket's prefix) and the bundled MinIO service can be
# removed from the stack. Two things become YOUR responsibility, because the
# bundled MinIO's defaults are what provide them here: the bucket must NOT be
# publicly readable (this platform never relies on anonymous access -- every
# read is presigned), and the credentials in ITS_S3_ACCESS_KEY/_SECRET_KEY
# should be an IAM identity scoped to this one bucket rather than an account
# root key, since every presigned URL is signed with them.

# --- Gateway ---
ITS_GATEWAY_URL=wss://your-host:8443/agent
# Remote-control relay: the agent dials this (over mTLS) for screen sessions.
# It is signed into every remote.screen.open job, so it must be the hostname
# agents resolve. Same passthrough port as ITS_GATEWAY_URL (8443); only the
# path differs (/relay vs /agent).
ITS_GATEWAY_RELAY_URL=wss://your-host:8443/relay
# Seconds without a heartbeat before the gateway considers an agent offline.
# Must stay comfortably above the agent's own heartbeat interval.
ITS_GATEWAY_HEARTBEAT_TIMEOUT_S=180

# --- Console address (email links, Microsoft 365 sign-in, push install) ---
# The https address people open the console at, scheme included and no
# trailing slash, e.g. https://itms.toeshee.com. SET IT ON EVERY REAL
# DEPLOYMENT. Four things read it:
#   * Push install (Windows, Linux and macOS): a pushed machine is told to
#     enroll here. EVERY PUSH INSTALL IS REFUSED WITHOUT IT -- recorded as
#     "Set ITS_PUBLIC_CONSOLE_URL so a pushed computer knows where to
#     enroll." -- and it must be the bare https origin (a host and an
#     optional port, no path), because that is what the installed agent's
#     ITS_AGENT_ENROLL_URL holds.
#   * The Group Policy MSI: the console address inside it, under the same
#     rule (a bare https origin on a DNS name). "Download MSI for Group
#     Policy" answers 503 without it; it never falls back to the address the
#     browser used, because the MSI goes to every computer in the domain.
#   * Keycloak, as KC_HOSTNAME: the address in every link it emails --
#     invitations and password resets. Left blank, those links name the
#     internal address http://keycloak:8080, which no recipient can open.
#   * "Connect with Microsoft" on Email Intake, as the sign-in return address.
#     It is a setting rather than read from the request because a return
#     address taken from the Host header can be forged. The same value with
#     /settings/email-intake/oauth/callback appended must be registered as a
#     redirect URI on the Azure application.
ITS_PUBLIC_CONSOLE_URL=

# --- AI apps over MCP: self-registration (off unless you opt in) ---
# Uncommented, this opens AI-app self-registration: any MCP client may then
# register itself in the identity provider (it still needs a person to sign in
# and approve it). Read deploy/README.md "AI connections (MCP)" first. Takes
# effect on the next keycloak-init run, and needs ITS_PUBLIC_CONSOLE_URL too.
# ITS_MCP_REGISTRATION=open

# --- Agent release-signing public key (Group Policy MSI) ---
# The PUBLIC half of the offline key agent releases are signed with, base64:
# the `release-signing-key.pub` asset of any agent release on GitHub (the
# release notes print it too). Not a secret. "Download MSI for Group Policy"
# verifies the agent it wraps against it, and refuses while it is unset.
ITS_AGENT_RELEASE_PUBLIC_KEY=

# --- Trusted proxy (install link) ---
# The only peers whose X-Forwarded-For header the API believes when it decides
# whether an install-link request comes from a network you allowed. Leave it
# unset: docker-compose.yml defaults it to ITMS_NETWORK_SUBNET, the network
# Traefik sits on. The API peels exactly one hop -- it takes the rightmost
# X-Forwarded-For entry, the address Traefik saw -- so only the bundled Traefik
# hop is supported. A further proxy or load balancer in front of ITMS is not
# supported for the install link's network check: Traefik would see that
# proxy, not the computer, and the check would compare the proxy's address.
# Prefixes separated by commas, host bits zero.
# ITS_TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS=172.19.240.0/24

# --- Job engine ---
# Seconds a dispatched job stays valid before the expiry sweep fails it, and
# how many delivery attempts a job gets before it is given up on. These bound
# every module's dispatch (patch, deploy, backup, provisioning, remote).
ITS_JOB_DEFAULT_TTL_S=3600
ITS_JOB_MAX_ATTEMPTS=3

# --- Remote control ---
# Screen share needs no key of its own here: the relay rides the gateway's
# :8443 (ITS_GATEWAY_RELAY_URL) and the internal listener is set in
# docker-compose.yml. guacd is retired; any ITS_REMOTE_GUACD_* key left in an
# existing .env is ignored and can be deleted.

# --- Subscriptions ---
# Days ahead the daily sweep announces cancellation-notice deadlines and
# renewals through notification (1-365). Optional; defaults to 30.
ITS_SUBSCRIPTION_ALERT_WINDOW_DAYS=30

# --- Patch Management ---
# Scan and apply operations run as Celery-beat-driven sweeps (no new compose
# services required). The default patch scan cadence is 24 hours per device-group
# (configured in patch_policies.scan_interval_hours; org-default applies to
# ungrouped devices). The scan sweep runs hourly to check if any device needs a
# scan; the apply sweep runs every 15 minutes to dispatch approved patches
# whose maintenance window is open. These sweep intervals (hourly check, 15-min
# check) are distinct from per-policy cadence — each device scans according to
# its resolved policy, not the sweep interval.
#
# macOS Apple-Silicon caveat: OS-update enforcement requires MDM, which is a
# fast-follow after v1. Version 1 does best-effort softwareupdate on Apple
# Silicon; full enforcement is not available until the MDM module ships.

# --- Application Deployment ---
# Application deployment (install/update/uninstall and signed custom scripts)
# runs as Celery-beat-driven sweeps (no new compose services required). The
# deploy-window-sweep beat runs every 15 minutes (900s) to dispatch scheduled
# deployments whose maintenance window is currently open.
#
# Package artifact storage: installers are stored in object storage (MinIO, the
# existing ObjectStore), referenced by artifact_ref in the packages table.
# Manager-sourced installers (winget/choco/brew) have no artifact (artifact_ref
# NULL). The agent downloads the artifact and verifies artifact_sha256 before
# execution; a mismatch fails the job without executing.
#
# Site-server LAN cache seam: in future phases (Phase 7 provisioning), a
# site-server may cache artifacts for LAN-heavy deployments. The agent would
# prefer a site-server cache when present, else fetch the artifact_ref from
# object storage. This is a documented seam only — not wired in v1 (no
# cache-location field exists on the job params yet).
#
# Deployments are RBAC-gated (deploy.create / deploy.execute permissions) and
# operator-initiated; there is no approval-ring workflow for deploy (unlike
# patch management). Custom script bodies travel inside the Ed25519-signed job
# envelope, so the agent verifies the signature before running as SYSTEM/root.

# --- Change management ---
# Where a submitted change opens its ticket. "service_desk" (the default)
# opens a Service Desk ticket and links it to the change; "null" turns that
# off (no ticket is opened). Control-plane only: no agent capability and no
# new compose services.
ITS_CHANGE_TICKET_BACKEND=service_desk

# --- BCP / DR test reports ---
# Control-plane only: the business-continuity / DR module (plans, manual test
# recording, findings, SOC 2 reports) adds NO new env vars, NO agent capability,
# NO worker sweeps, and NO new compose services. It reuses Postgres, the audit
# log, and RBAC, and reads restore-test evidence (metadata only) from the backup
# module. Nothing to configure here.

# --- Licenses (SAM) ---
# Control-plane only: the Licenses / software-asset-management module (vendors,
# products, licenses, allocations, compliance reconciliation, reports) adds NO
# new env vars, NO agent capability, and NO new compose services. It reuses
# Postgres, the vault (per-org envelope seals license keys; reveal is audited
# via licenses.keys.read), the audit log, and the existing Celery/Redis worker
# for the daily `licenses-compliance-sweep`. Per-record currency, no FX (Budget
# owns cross-currency roll-up). Nothing to configure here.

# --- Subscriptions (SaaS management) ---
# Control-plane only: the Subscriptions module (SaaS/seat registry, renewal and
# cancellation-notice tracking, seat pools, spend reports) adds NO new env vars,
# NO agent capability, NO worker sweeps, and NO new compose services. Unlike
# Licenses it has no Celery beat job at all — notice deadlines and renewal flags
# are computed at read time, so there is no push/email channel in v1. It reuses
# Postgres, the audit log, RBAC, and the shared `vendor` entity from Licenses.
# Per-record currency, no FX (Budget owns cross-currency roll-up). `payment_ref`
# is a plaintext reference string, NOT a secret — never put card numbers or
# credentials in it. Nothing to configure here.

# --- Notification ---
# Control-plane only: the Notification module adds NO env vars, NO agent
# capability, and NO new compose services. It reuses Postgres (the outbox and
# `notification_deliveries`), the audit log, RBAC, and the existing Celery/
# Redis worker. Two beat entries run in the `worker` service, both every 30s:
# `notification-drain-outbox` (domain events -> delivery rows) and
# `notification-deliver-pending` (attempt/retry with backoff).
# Per-channel configuration is DATA, not environment: an org's SMTP/webhook
# endpoint lives on a `notification_channels` row and its secret is
# envelope-sealed in the vault (invariant #3) -- there is deliberately no
# global ITS_SMTP_* or ITS_WEBHOOK_* setting to configure here. Outbound
# webhook destinations are constrained by the `webhook_allowlist_entries`
# table, also per-org data.

# --- Email intake ---
# Control-plane only: NO env vars, NO agent capability, NO new compose
# services. One beat entry in the `worker` service, `email-intake-poll`, every
# 120s; it polls each org's configured mailboxes over IMAP.
# Mailbox host/port/username live on an `email_mailbox` row and the IMAP
# PASSWORD is envelope-sealed in the vault -- never in this file. The poller's
# per-socket IMAP timeout (30s) and its conversation deadline are code
# constants, not settings.
# Egress note: this is the only module that makes OUTBOUND connections to an
# operator-supplied host from the `worker` container. Allow the worker egress
# to your mail server's IMAPS port.

# --- SLA engine ---
# Control-plane only: NO env vars, NO agent capability, NO new compose
# services. One beat entry in the `worker` service, `sla-sweep`, whose
# schedule is `app.modules.sla.sweep.SWEEP_INTERVAL_SECONDS` -- a code
# constant (300s), NOT an environment setting, so the beat entry and the
# sweep's own retry reasoning can never drift apart. Do not add an env var
# for it without changing both.
# Everything an operator actually tunes is PER-ORG DATA, not configuration:
# business calendars and holidays (`sla_calendar*`), targets per (queue,
# priority) (`sla_policy`), and auto-close-after-days (`sla_org_settings`,
# default 7 when an org has no row). Configure them through the API/UI.

# --- Endpoint health & alerting ---
# NO env vars, NO new compose services, NO new volume or secret. It DOES add
# agent capabilities (`health.policy.apply`, `health.check.now`) -- the entry
# above this one says "no agent capability" and this one deliberately does not.
# Five beat entries run in the `worker` service; every interval is a code
# constant read from `app.modules.health.sweep` (`ACTION_INTERVAL_S` 30s,
# `ALERT_SWEEP_INTERVAL_S` 60s, `OFFLINE_SWEEP_INTERVAL_S` 60s,
# `POLICY_RECONCILE_INTERVAL_S` 900s, `RETENTION_INTERVAL_S` daily), NOT an
# environment setting: how often we look is a property of this deployment, and
# a per-org interval lets one org's setting delay another org's escalation.
# What IS per-org is the THRESHOLD each sweep compares against --
# `escalate_after_s`, `offline_after_s`, `alert_retention_days`, check
# definitions and notification routing -- all of it rows, configured through
# the API/UI. There is nothing to set here; this section exists so the next
# reader knows that was checked rather than forgotten.

# --- IPAM ---
# NO env vars, NO new compose services, NO new volume or secret. It DOES add an
# agent capability (`net.sweep`), and that capability is the reason the
# SCHEDULE IS SERVER-SIDE: the authorized target list is computed at dispatch
# time and carried inside the signed params, so an agent-local cron would be a
# standing authorization no revocation could stop (invariant #1). Beat entries
# run in the `worker` service and their schedules are code constants read from
# the module -- `ipam.sweep.SWEEP_DISPATCH_INTERVAL_S` (hourly; it asks what is
# DUE, it does not sweep every subnet every hour) and
# `ipam.reconcile.RECONCILE_INTERVAL_SECONDS` (daily, because drift needs two
# consecutive detections and the cadence IS the deduplication window).
# Everything an operator tunes is PER-ORG DATA: VRFs, subnets, per-subnet
# `sweep_enabled`/`sweep_interval_hours`, and the AUTHORIZED SCOPES a sweep is
# allowed to probe. An unauthorized subnet is refused before anything is
# signed; there is no env var that widens that, deliberately.

# --- AI admin agent ---
# THE ONLY MODULE PAST SLA THAT ADDS ENV VARS, and they are dynamic names
# rather than pydantic `Settings` fields, so `app/core/config.py` does not
# list them: `app.modules.ai_admin.providers.load_provider_configs` reads
# `os.environ` directly. That is why they are written out here in full.
#
# THIS BLOCK IS THE ON SWITCH, AND IT IS THE ONLY ONE (operator decision,
# 2026-08-25; docs/modules/ai-admin/spec.md §0.14). Set these five variables and
# the assistant is available to every organization on this deployment, subject
# only to the `ai.use` / `ai.admin` permissions. Leave them unset and it is
# available to none: `load_provider_configs` returns an empty map, `GET
# /ai/status` reports the feature unavailable, and the turn route answers 503.
# There is no bundled key, no default base URL and no default model.
#
# THERE IS NO LONGER A PER-ORG SWITCH AND NO PROVIDER DROPDOWN. The console
# neither picks a provider nor displays one to choose from, and `PUT
# /ai/settings` has no `enabled`, no `provider_key` and no `egress_acknowledged`
# field -- migration 0074 dropped the columns. What an org-admin still tunes is
# retention, the two rate ceilings and the token budget.
#
# EXACTLY ONE PROVIDER MAY BE NAMED. "The same model for every tenant" and a
# list of two cannot both be true, so ITS_AI_PROVIDERS holding two keys is a
# configuration error raised AT STARTUP, naming both. This deployment runs one
# model for everybody on it; a customer who needs a different one gets a
# different deployment, which is how this platform is delivered anyway.
#
# A named provider with a MISSING or MISSPELLED variable raises at
# configuration read time, naming the variable -- not at 09:00 when an
# administrator asks a question. `create_app` forces that read at startup, so a
# broken block fails in your terminal, next to the file you just edited.
#
# ITS_AI_PROVIDERS=local              # ONE provider key. Two is an error.
# ITS_AI_LOCAL_KIND=openai_compatible # "openai_compatible" | "anthropic"
# ITS_AI_LOCAL_BASE_URL=http://vllm.internal:8000/v1
# ITS_AI_LOCAL_MODEL=<model name>
# ITS_AI_LOCAL_API_KEY_PATH=/var/lib/its/secrets/ai-provider.key   # optional
#
# `<KEY>` in `ITS_AI_<KEY>_*` is the uppercased provider key from
# ITS_AI_PROVIDERS. Rename the provider and every variable renames with it: a
# key of `hosted` reads ITS_AI_HOSTED_KIND, ITS_AI_HOSTED_BASE_URL, and so on.
#
# THE KEY PATH MUST BE ON THE `its-secrets` VOLUME (mounted at
# /var/lib/its/secrets in the `api` service), the same convention
# ITS_AUDIT_HMAC_KEY_PATH uses above. A path anywhere else is a
# FileNotFoundError at the first hosted-provider call, and -- worse -- key
# material outside the volume set docs/07-backup-restore.md §2.2 backs up.
# The file is read at point of use and never cached, so rotating it takes
# effect on the next call rather than at the next restart.
#
# The BASE URL IS NEVER ORG-EDITABLE and is not editable anywhere else either:
# it exists only here. That removes the SSRF primitive rather than guarding it.
# What remains PER-ORG DATA, configured through the API/UI under `ai.admin`, is
# `transcript_retention_days`, the two rate ceilings, `daily_token_budget`,
# `action_expiry_s` and `max_proposals_per_turn` -- cost and retention, never
# reach. One beat entry runs in the `worker` service,
# `ai-transcript-retention`, whose schedule is
# `app.modules.ai_admin.retention.RETENTION_INTERVAL_S` (daily); the WINDOW it
# applies is the per-org setting, not this interval.
#
# EGRESS, AND WHERE THE CONSENT NOW LIVES. Configuring a provider of kind
# `anthropic` means real customer data leaves your network -- the
# administrator's question text and tool results carrying hostnames, ticket
# subjects and bodies, software inventory, alert text, licence and vendor names,
# spend figures and audit action names. deploy/README.md enumerates exactly what
# does and does not leave. There used to be a tickbox in the console that an
# org-admin had to accept before enabling the feature; that gate is gone, and
# UNCOMMENTING THE BLOCK ABOVE IS NOW THE ACT OF CONSENT. It is deliberate,
# server-side, and reviewable in configuration management, which the tickbox was
# not. The console still shows the full disclosure of what leaves; the
# `ai.settings_changed` audit event records which provider this deployment was
# configured with at the time of each change.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OpenBao auto-unseal (OPTIONAL, OFF). Written ONLY by
# `./scripts/openbao-seal.sh enable`, after it has migrated the seal; adding
# these by hand leaves OpenBao unable to unseal. Read deploy/GO-LIVE.md §4
# (the at-rest vs availability trade-off) before enabling.
#
# COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:openbao/auto-unseal.yml
# OPENBAO_UNSEAL_KEY_FILE=/etc/it-suite/openbao-unseal/unseal.key
